.bd
SUBROUTINE(S)
.in +5
.br
.bd
fmtlong
- format a long into a string
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
char *fmtlong(buf, v)
.br
char *buf;
.br
long v;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Fmtlong
writes
.bd
v
into
.bd
buf
in decimal, with a leading minus if it is negative, and terminates
the result with a null.  It returns a pointer to that null - to the
end of what it wrote, not to the beginning - which is the contract
.bd
fmtstr (3)
keeps, so the two can be strung together to build one string without
walking it again:

.sp
.in +5
p = fmtlong(buf, value);
.br
p = fmtstr(p, " %s\n", name);
.in -5
.sp

This is a separate subroutine, and a separate member of the archive,
because it is the only thing in
.bd
libccc.a
that divides a long.  Held inside
.bd
fmtstr
as a
.bd
%ld
conversion it put that division - and the conversion itself - into
every program that formatted anything, which on this machine was
several hundred bytes each in c0, c1 and peep for a conversion only
cpp ever asked for.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
Nothing is bounded.  Twelve bytes are enough for any long this
machine can hold, sign and null included, and the caller is
responsible for them.

The most negative long has no positive counterpart, so negating it
leaves it negative and it is not converted correctly.  That is the
same limitation the
.bd
%ld
conversion had, carried over unchanged rather than fixed here.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
